#: src/macros/intro.md
msgid ""
"Before looking at macros in detail, it is helpful to build a conceptual model "
"of what macros are and how they fit into the compilation pipeline."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/intro.md
msgid "At their core, macros are **meta-programming**:"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/intro.md
msgid "They take input code (expressed as tokens) and emit output code."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/intro.md
msgid ""
"They execute during compilation, expanding before type checking and semantic "
"analysis."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/intro.md
msgid ""
"They allow expressing patterns and abstractions that cannot be written with "
"generics or standard functions."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/intro.md
msgid ""
"We will explore how compilers process source code, and how different macro "
"systems hook into this process."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/intro.md
msgid ""
"Ask students if they have used macros in other languages (e.g., C/C++ "
"preprocessor, Lisp, or Clojure)."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/intro/why.md
msgid ""
"Macros are a powerful tool, but carry their own complexity and should be used "
"only when worthwhile. They are used to solve problems that cannot be easily "
"solved with standard Rust language features:"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/intro/why.md
msgid ""
"**Reducing Boilerplate:** Implementing traits via `#[derive(...)]` or "
"reducing syntactic repetition."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/intro/why.md
msgid ""
"**Variadic Interfaces:** Functions that take a variable number of arguments "
"of different types (like `println!` and `vec!`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/intro/why.md
msgid ""
"**Domain-Specific Languages (DSLs):** Creating custom syntax inside Rust "
"(e.g., `println!`'s format strings, or macros that statically expand HTML "
"templates or parse SQL queries)."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/intro/why.md
msgid ""
"**Compile-Time Computation:** Inspecting configuration files, environment "
"variables, or data at build time (e.g., `include_str!`, `env!`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/intro/why.md
msgid ""
"Because each macro can define its own input syntax and can perform operations "
"not available to normal Rust code, macros can make code harder to read than "
"it would be otherwise. Try to avoid using macros when the same abstraction "
"can be achieved in another way, and when defining macros, try to align with "
"syntactic and naming patterns followed in non-macro Rust code."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/intro/why.md
msgid ""
"Contrast with limitations of normal Rust code: functions have a fixed "
"signature (fixed number of arguments of specific types) and cannot generate "
"new types or modules."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/intro/how.md
msgid ""
"To understand how macros work, it helps to look at the pipeline of how a "
"compiler processes your code:"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/intro/how.md
msgid ""
"**Source Code:** The raw text files written by the programmer, consisting of "
"a sequence of characters in some character set (in Rust, Unicode characters "
"in UTF-8)."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/intro/how.md
msgid ""
"**Lexical Analysis (Lexing):** The compiler delimits the raw text into a "
"sequence of **tokens** (e.g., `fn`, `main`, identifiers, punctuation, "
"literals)."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/intro/how.md
msgid ""
"**Syntactic Analysis (Parsing):** Based on the language's grammar, the "
"compiler organizes the sequence of tokens into an **Abstract Syntax Tree "
"(AST)** that captures the hierarchical structure of the program."
msgstr ""

