#: src/macros/kinds.md
msgid ""
"Macros can be attached to or invoked at different positions in your code. "
"There are three major cases to consider:"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds.md
msgid ""
"**Function-Like Macros:** Invoked as expressions, statements, types, "
"patterns, or items, expanding to any syntax that could occupy the invocation "
"position."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds.md
msgid ""
"**Derive Macros:** Attached specifically to `struct`, `enum`, or `union` "
"items, to generate trait impls based on the type definition."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds.md
msgid ""
"**Attribute Macros:** Attached to any standard Rust `item` (including "
"functions, modules, structs, etc.), allowing complete replacement of the "
"attached item."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds.md
msgid "We will now see how each of these looks and behaves."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds.md
msgid ""
"Mention that \"items\" in Rust are top-level declarations (which can nest!) "
"such as structs, modules, traits, and functions."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds.md
msgid ""
"Clarify that some macro positions are more restricted than others: e.g., "
"derive macros cannot modify the structs they are attached to, but only append "
"new implementations alongside; meanwhile, attribute macros can rewrite their "
"targets entirely."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds/function-like.md
msgid ""
"**Function-like macros** are invoked with an exclamation mark (`!`) following "
"their identifier. They look like function calls, but they can accept "
"arbitrary tokens inside their brackets."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds/function-like.md
msgid "// Invoked as an expression:\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds/function-like.md
msgid "// Invoked as a statement:\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds/function-like.md
#, fuzzy
msgid "\"Hello, World! {:?}\""
msgstr "Hello World!"

#: src/macros/kinds/function-like.md
msgid ""
"**Flexible brackets:** Can be invoked with parentheses `()`, square brackets "
"`[]`, or curly braces `{}`."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds/function-like.md
msgid ""
"**Input:** Can contain any sequence of Rust tokens, but unmatched opening/"
"closing brackets are forbidden (so the compiler can tell where the macro "
"invocation ends, regardless of how the macro processes those tokens)."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds/function-like.md
msgid ""
"**Placement:** Can appear as expressions, statements, types, patterns, or "
"items."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds/function-like.md
msgid ""
"**Implementation:** These are the only kind of macro that can be implemented "
"as either a **declarative** macro (`macro_rules!`) or a **procedural** macro."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds/function-like.md
msgid ""
"Mention that while they look like function calls, they are expanded during "
"compilation. They do not exist at runtime."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds/function-like.md
msgid ""
"The choice of brackets is a matter of stylistic convention: `vec!` uses `[]` "
"because it behaves like an array, `println!` uses `()` because it behaves "
"like a function call, and custom macros defining items often use `{}`."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds/derive.md
msgid ""
"**Derive macros** are attached to structs, enums, or unions by preceding them "
"with the `#[derive(...)]` attribute. They generate new code next to the type, "
"typically implementing traits automatically."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds/derive.md
msgid "\"Default result: {:?}\""
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds/derive.md
msgid ""
"**Targeted:** Can only be applied to type definitions using the `struct`, "
"`enum`, or `union` keywords."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds/derive.md
msgid ""
"**Additive:** They cannot modify or delete the original type definition; they "
"can only output additional code (such as `impl` blocks)."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds/derive.md src/macros/kinds/attr.md
msgid "**Implementation:** Always implemented as procedural macros."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds/derive.md
msgid ""
"Explain that `#[derive(Debug)]` generates an implementation of "
"`std::fmt::Debug` for the struct."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds/derive.md
msgid ""
"Note that derive macros can also support \"helper attributes\" (attributes "
"placed on struct fields, like `#[serde(rename = \"name\")]` or `#[default]` "
"in the example), which configure the generated code."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds/derive.md
msgid ""
"Mention that derive macros are compiled together in the same module as the "
"type. Any items they generate (like helper functions or helper traits) exist "
"in the same namespace, so they must be designed carefully to avoid namespace "
"collisions."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds/attr.md
msgid ""
"**Attribute macros** are attached as custom attributes to any standard Rust "
"`item` (including functions, modules, structs, etc.). They have full power to "
"inspect, rewrite, or completely replace the item they are attached to."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds/attr.md
msgid "// A popular example from the Tokio library:\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds/attr.md
msgid "\"Hello from an async main!\""
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds/attr.md
msgid "**Universal:** Can be applied to any declaration/item."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds/attr.md
msgid ""
"**Transformational:** Unlike derive macros, they can entirely replace their "
"target with different code."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds/attr.md
msgid ""
"**Parameters:** Can optionally accept custom metadata parameters, e.g., "
"`#[route(GET, \"/\")]`."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds/attr.md
msgid ""
"Discuss how `#[tokio::main]` converts an `async fn main()` into a synchronous "
"`fn main()` that instantiates and runs the Tokio runtime, blocks on the "
"future, and executes the async block. This completely rewrites the function "
"signature!"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/kinds/attr.md
msgid ""
"Emphasize that because attribute macros can entirely rewrite items, they are "
"extremely powerful but require careful error handling to avoid completely "
"obscuring compile errors for the user."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/tokens.md
msgid ""
"Rather than manipulating raw text, Rust macros operate on **tokens** and "
"**token trees**."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/tokens.md
msgid ""
"**Tokens** are the smallest indivisible lexical elements of the language "
"(e.g., identifiers like `foo`, operators like `+`, punctuation like `,`, and "
"literals like `123`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/tokens.md
msgid ""
"**Token trees** are formed by grouping streams of tokens and token groups."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/tokens.md
msgid "When a macro is called, the compiler passes it such a stream."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/tokens.md
msgid ""
"The macro processes this input stream and must return another stream of "
"tokens-and-groups that replaces the macro invocation."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/tokens.md
msgid ""
"This structure allows the compiler to validate lexical boundaries and ensures "
"that macros can never construct or return syntactically invalid, un-lexable "
"text."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/tokens.md
msgid ""
"Reiterate that because macro expansion happens _during_ AST generation, the "
"macro receives structured tokens, not characters."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/tokens.md
msgid ""
"Explain that this differs completely from preprocessors (like C/C++), which "
"perform string/text-level substitution before tokenization."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/tokens.md
msgid ""
"Mention that this token-level manipulation is what makes Rust macros more "
"robust and syntax-safe."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/brackets.md
msgid ""
"A **token tree** can represent either a single token, or a **group** of "
"tokens enclosed by matching delimiters (brackets):"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/brackets.md
msgid "**Single tokens (leaf nodes):** `foo`, `+`, `,`, `123`."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/brackets.md
msgid ""
"**Grouped tokens (internal nodes):** Enclosed by parentheses `()`, braces `{}"
"`, or square brackets `[]`."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/brackets.md
msgid "For example, the token stream: `foo + (bar * baz)`"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/brackets.md
msgid "Is parsed into **3 separate token trees**:"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/brackets.md
msgid "`foo` (a single token)"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/brackets.md
msgid "`+` (a single token)"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/brackets.md
msgid ""
"`(bar * baz)` (a token group containing three child token trees: `bar`, `*`, "
"and `baz`)"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/brackets.md
msgid ""
"Because grouping happens _during_ lexical analysis, **unbalanced groups are "
"strictly disallowed**. You cannot pass unbalanced parentheses or braces into "
"or out of a macro!"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/brackets.md
msgid ""
"Note that this means you cannot use a macro to generate half a block, like "
"`let x = {` and close it with another macro or tokens outside the macro. The "
"entire block must be passed or returned as a single, well-formed token group."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/streams.md
msgid ""
"A **token stream** is a sequence of token trees. It is the fundamental input "
"and output format for both declarative and procedural macros in Rust:"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/streams.md
msgid ""
"**Macro Input:** The tokens passed inside the brackets of the macro "
"invocation."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/streams.md
msgid ""
"**Macro Output:** The new tokens returned by the macro that replace the macro "
"invocation."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/streams.md
msgid ""
"For declarative macros, the output is structured not just as a token stream, "
"but also must be syntactically valid Rust. The generated syntax is inserted "
"into the AST of the program where the macro is invoked."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/streams.md
msgid ""
"// The input token stream does not need to be valid Rust syntax, as long as "
"it\n"
"// tokenizes (which requires any brackets to be balanced).\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/streams.md
msgid ""
"// The macro returns tokens: 1 + 2\n"
"    // Since expansion inserts a structured AST fragment, precedence is "
"preserved!\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/streams.md
msgid "\"3 * 10 = {}\""
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/streams.md
msgid "// Outputs \"30\"\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/streams.md
msgid ""
"Because macro output is inserted as an AST subtree, we don't need to wrap "
"expansions in safety parentheses (unlike C macros, where literal replacement "
"would cause `1 + 2 * 10` to equal `21`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/streams.md
msgid ""
"This slide shows `macro_rules!` defining a declarative macro. We do not need "
"to visit this in detail yet, as we will do so on the next slide."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/streams.md
msgid ""
"Explain that a Token Stream can contain arbitrary sequences of tokens. The "
"tokens do not need to form valid Rust code on their own (for example, "
"`special_print!(#foo(bar:123))` uses `#` and `:` in custom ways)."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/streams.md
msgid ""
"When a macro expands, the _emitted_ token stream must make syntactic sense at "
"the position where the macro is invoked."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/welcome.md
msgid ""
"**Macros by Example** (also known as **Declarative Macros** or `macro_rules!` "
"macros) are the most common form of macros in Rust and the easiest to write."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/welcome.md
msgid "They are defined using the `macro_rules!` construct."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/welcome.md
msgid ""
"They allow you to write custom pattern matches on tokens, expanding them into "
"standard Rust code."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/welcome.md
msgid ""
"They are partially hygienic: local variables and labels defined inside them "
"do not leak outside."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/welcome.md
msgid ""
"They introduce minimal compile-time overhead and have no external crate "
"dependencies."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/welcome.md
msgid ""
"We will learn how to write, configure, and match patterns in declarative "
"macros."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/welcome.md
msgid ""
"Mention that declarative macros are sometimes called \"macros by example\" "
"because you write an example of the syntax you want to match, and an example "
"of the output you want to produce. This is much like C preprocessor macros, "
"but with richer pattern matching."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/welcome.md
msgid ""
"Highlight that they are defined directly in your standard code, making them "
"convenient to add without extra setup."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/welcome.md
msgid ""
"This slide acts as a transition into how declarative macros are defined and "
"used."
msgstr ""

