#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques.md
msgid ""
"As your declarative macros become more sophisticated, simple patterns may not "
"be enough to express your intended logic."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques.md
msgid ""
"Rust programmers have established several design patterns for structuring "
"more complex macros:"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques.md
msgid ""
"**Optional Parameters:** Using multiple match arms or optional matchers to "
"handle default values."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques.md
msgid ""
"**Named Parameters:** Explicitly labeling parameters at the callsite (e.g., "
"`key=value`) to improve readability."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques.md
msgid ""
"**Internal Rules:** Prefixing helper match arms with a unique symbol (like "
"`@`) to modularize code."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques.md
msgid ""
"**Incremental Token Munchers:** Eating tokens off the front of a stream "
"recursively."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques.md
msgid ""
"**Push-Down Accumulators:** Collecting tokens recursively into a buffer to "
"build a structured output."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques.md
msgid ""
"**TT Bundling:** Grouping multiple arguments inside a single Token Tree to "
"simplify passing them down."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques.md
msgid "We will consider each of these advanced techniques in turn."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques.md
msgid ""
"Reassure students that these patterns are standard idioms in advanced Rust "
"code bases, but not necessary for simple macros. A programmer must use their "
"discretion to choose between a complex declarative macro and implementing "
"logic in a procedural macro, which may be more straightforward at the cost of "
"additional ceremony."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques.md
msgid ""
"Explain that because declarative macros don't have standard local variables "
"or mutable state during expansion, recursion and token-level manipulation are "
"the primary ways to achieve complex code generation."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/optional-params.md
msgid ""
"The `?` repetition operator can be used for optional parameters, but in older "
"Rust editions (2018 and 2015) it must be emulated; this can be done by "
"defining multiple matcher arms that cascade. Their right-hand sides may reuse "
"each other via recursion, which is a common pattern in declarative macros "
"regardless of edition."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/optional-params.md
msgid "// Single parameter:\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/optional-params.md
msgid ""
"// Recurse instead of `println!(\"{}:{}: {}\", file!(), line!(), $msg)`:\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/optional-params.md
msgid "// With optional formatting parameters:\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/optional-params.md
msgid "\"{}:{}: \""
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/optional-params.md
msgid "\"my message\""
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/optional-params.md
msgid "\"my message with param {}!\""
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/optional-params.md
msgid ""
"Highlight how `concat!` is used at compile time to combine the file/line "
"prefix with the user's log message literal."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/optional-params.md
msgid ""
"Recall that the order of arms is significant, just like in a `match`. The "
"compiler matches sequentially, and if a broader arm is placed above a "
"narrower one, the narrower arm may never be reached."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/named-params.md
msgid ""
"Using literal identifiers as key-value descriptors allows you to build "
"declarative macro APIs with named parameters. For macros with many arguments, "
"this may significantly increase readability at the callsite:"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/named-params.md
msgid "// Callers pass named parameters:\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/named-params.md
msgid "// Calls zero()\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/named-params.md
msgid "// Calls clear()\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/named-params.md
msgid ""
"**Structural Identifiers:** `type:` and `function:` are matched as literal "
"tokens."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/named-params.md
msgid ""
"**Improved Intent:** The callsite clearly documents which identifier is the "
"type and which is the method, instead of relying on positional parameter "
"index ordering."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/internal-rules.md
msgid ""
"A common pattern for implementing complex macros is to define helper arms "
"(often called **internal rules**) prefixed with a unique, non-standard token, "
"such as `@`."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/internal-rules.md
msgid ""
"These internal arms are not meant to be called by users, but are used "
"internally by the macro's public entry points to delegate and reuse logic:"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/internal-rules.md
msgid "// Internal helper rules:\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/internal-rules.md
msgid "\"all-lit: {}\""
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/internal-rules.md
msgid "\"some-lit: {}\""
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/internal-rules.md
msgid "// Public entrypoints:\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/internal-rules.md
msgid "\"format: {} {}\""
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/internal-rules.md
msgid "\"wow\""
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/internal-rules.md
msgid "\"non-literal\""
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/internal-rules.md
msgid ""
"Note that `@` is purely a convention, which works well because `@` cannot "
"begin a standard Rust item or expression, ensuring public users don't "
"accidentally invoke these helper arms."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/internal-rules.md
msgid ""
"Trace the public matcher arms' flow through the recursive call to "
"(`print_literals!`) while selecting the next branch to take using the `@`\\-"
"prefixed tokens."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/token-munchers.md
msgid ""
"An **Incremental TT Muncher** is a recursive macro pattern that processes "
"input token streams by \"eating\" (matching) one or more tokens off the front "
"of the stream, and recursively passing the remainder back to itself."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/token-munchers.md
msgid "// Base case: Only one method call remains\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/token-munchers.md
#, fuzzy
msgid "\"Hello, World\""
msgstr "Hello World!"

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/token-munchers.md
msgid "// Expands recursively to: s.len().trailing_zeros()\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/token-munchers.md
msgid "\"Trailing zeros in length: {}\""
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/token-munchers.md
msgid ""
"TT munchers can parse complex custom grammars, but on the other hand, they "
"can be difficult to read and debug. For small embedded DSLs, they are "
"probably the right choice, but for more complex parsers other techniques may "
"be more appropriate."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/token-munchers.md
msgid "Limitations"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/token-munchers.md
msgid ""
"Because the recursive call passes down the entire rest of the input, while "
"each step only removes a fixed-size portion of it, TT munchers are quadratic "
"in the size of their input. For large inputs, compile times can bloat, and "
"you might exceed the compiler's default macro recursion limit."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/token-munchers.md
msgid ""
"There are also some limits around the compiler's built-in follow-set "
"restrictions (on which fragment specifiers are allowed immediately preceding "
"a trailing `tt`), needed to prevent parser ambiguity. If we follow the "
"pattern of sticking to `tt` matchers on the left, we mostly avoid this "
"limitation."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/token-munchers.md
msgid ""
"Explain that the `tt` specifier is key here because it matches any single "
"token tree (including identifiers, literals, or bracketed groups), allowing "
"the recursive call to accept \"anything else\" in `$tail`."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/token-munchers.md
msgid ""
"Note how `chain!(s, len, trailing_zeros)` matches the recursive case, "
"expanding to `chain!(s.len(), trailing_zeros)`, which then matches the base "
"case, expanding to `s.len().trailing_zeros()`."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/token-munchers.md
msgid ""
"Warn about rule ordering: a repeated `tt` matcher will also match a single "
"`tt`, so rules expecting the latter should come first."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/techniques/token-munchers.md
msgid ""
"Discuss tradeoffs: if a macro is complex enough to require a TT muncher, "
"there is a chance it may be better expressed as a procedural macro."
msgstr ""

