#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/dyn/any-trait.md
msgid "Any Trait and Downcasting"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/dyn/any-trait.md
msgid "// We can get a unique identifier for the type.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/dyn/any-trait.md
msgid "// We can check if our object is a particular type.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/dyn/any-trait.md
msgid "// We can attempt to downcast to a concrete type.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/dyn/any-trait.md
msgid ""
"By default, trait objects cannot be downcasted to their concrete type. This "
"is because they do not have any runtime type information that would allow us "
"to determine what the concrete type is, and Rust won't allow us to blindly "
"cast to a type that may not be correct."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/dyn/any-trait.md
msgid ""
"The `Any` trait allows us to downcast values back from dyn values into "
"concrete values by adding the necessary runtime type information."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/dyn/any-trait.md
msgid ""
"This is an auto trait: like Send/Sync/Sized, it is automatically implemented "
"for any type that meets specific criteria."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/dyn/any-trait.md
msgid ""
"The criteria for Any is that a type is `'static`. That is, the type does not "
"contain any non-`'static` lifetimes within it."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/dyn/any-trait.md
msgid ""
"Any offers two related behaviors: downcasting, and runtime checking of types "
"being the same."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/dyn/any-trait.md
msgid ""
"In the example above, we see the ability to downcast from `Any` into "
"`ThisImplementsAny` automatically."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/dyn/any-trait.md
msgid "We also see `Any::is` being used to check to see what type the value is."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/dyn/any-trait.md
msgid ""
"`Any` does not implement reflection for a type, it just adds the runtime type "
"information necessary to safely determine the concrete type at runtime."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/dyn/any-trait.md
msgid ""
"You can add downcasting support to your own traits by using `Any` as a "
"supertrait."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/dyn/any-trait.md
msgid ""
"This also works for generics! We can use `Any` as a trait bound in our "
"generic code, and then \"downcast\" to a concrete type. This is uncommon, "
"though, as generics already have full type information and access to all "
"functionality provided by traits."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/dyn/sealing-with-enums.md
msgid ""
"Motivation: API is designed around a specific list of types that are valid "
"for it, users of the API are not expected to extend it."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/dyn/sealing-with-enums.md
msgid ""
"Enums in Rust are _algebraic data types_, we can define different structures "
"for each variant."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/dyn/sealing-with-enums.md
msgid ""
"For some domains, this might be enough polymorphism for the problem. "
"Experiment and see what works, what solutions seem to make more sense."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/dyn/sealing-with-enums.md
msgid ""
"By having the user-facing part of the API refer to an enum, users know what "
"types are valid inputs and can construct those types using the available "
"methods to do so."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/dyn/sealing-with-enums.md
msgid ""
"If the types that make up the enum have invariants that the API internally "
"upholds, and the only way users can construct those types is through "
"constructors that build and maintain those invariants, then you can be sure "
"that inputs to a generic method uphold their invariants."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/dyn/sealing-with-enums.md
msgid ""
"If the types that make up the enum instead are types the user can freely "
"construct, then sanitisation and interpretation may need to be taken into "
"consideration."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enum-vs-dyn.md
msgid ""
"Enums and `dyn` provide two different approaches to dynamic polymorphism, and "
"in many cases both approaches can be used to solve the same problem. The main "
"trade-offs between the two are:"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enum-vs-dyn.md
msgid ""
"Enums make \"downcasting\" to a subtype easy via pattern matching. "
"Downcasting is possible with `dyn` but is more cumbersome."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enum-vs-dyn.md
msgid ""
"`dyn` allows for downstream code to introduce new types, whereas enums do not."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enum-vs-dyn.md
msgid ""
"When deciding whether to use an enum or `dyn`, there are two questions to ask:"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enum-vs-dyn.md
msgid ""
"Do I need to be able to downcast to the concrete subtype? Or do I primarily "
"expect to go through a trait interface without needing to know the concrete "
"type?"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enum-vs-dyn.md
msgid ""
"Do I know the full set of types up front, or do I need to allow downstream "
"code to extend the set of types I will be handling?"
msgstr ""

